WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
Your receptionist is
helping a patient.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:02.500
The phone rings.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.833
Later, the practice closes.

00:00:04.233 --> 00:00:04.900
Someone else calls.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.433
What happens to those enquiries?

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:08.533
Watch how a call
can reach a booking,

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:10.600
while your team stays in control.

00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:14.200
FlowAudit builds AI phone
agents that support your team

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.567
through agreed workflows.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.500
You agree when calls are forwarded,

00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:19.433
which appointments
the agent can handle,

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.300
and when a person needs to step in.

00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:22.700
Here’s a recorded demo.

00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:24.933
A new patient asks for a check-up.

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:27.167
Hiya, I just wanted to ring
in and book an appointment.

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:29.267
The agent offers available times.

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:32.233
The first available
appointment this afternoon

00:00:32.233 --> 00:00:34.533
is at 2:30 or three in the afternoon

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:35.800
or 3:30.

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:37.333
Which one works for you?

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:38.700
Can we do three o’clock?

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.400
The caller chooses three o’clock.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.900
And here is the booking
in Google Calendar.

00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:45.500
Your new patient
check-up appointment

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:46.833
is booked for Wednesday,

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:48.300
the 7th of October

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.767
at three in the afternoon.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.433
That’s the workflow to assess:

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.700
give routine enquiries
a clear next step,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.200
during busy periods and after hours,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.600
without pulling your team
away from the patient in front

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.067
of them.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.567
Start with your own call records.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:02.533
Which enquiries need a reply,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.700
and which can your team
already handle?

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:05.633
Before setup,

00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:08.333
we check your scheduling,
forwarding and patient-data

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:08.867
requirements.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.167
Clinical judgment
stays with your team.

00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:13.033
You agree scope and investment.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.800
Agreement and initial
payment start configuration,

00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:17.967
followed by testing with your team.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:19.767
Activation follows your approval.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.467
Book a fifteen-minute
demo and fit assessment.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.767
Bring the person who
approves the purchase.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.500
We’ll look at your call
workflows and decide whether

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.600
there’s a useful fit.
